WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.040
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:11.079
CAD stands for computer aided design, basically
a digital 3D model of a scene.

00:00:11.079 --> 00:00:16.209
Here is an incredibly difficult problem: what
if we give the computer a photograph of a

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:21.490
living room, and the output would be a digital,
fully modeled 3D scene.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:22.990
A CAD model.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:26.800
This is a remarkably difficult problem: just
think about it!

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.930
The algorithm would have to have an understanding
of perspective, illumination, occlusions,

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:34.020
and geometry.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.040
And with that in mind, have a look at these
incredible results.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.180
Now, this clearly sounds impossible.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:46.180
Not so long ago we talked about a neural network-based
technique that tried to achieve something

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:52.240
similar and it was remarkable, but the output
was a low resolution voxel array, which is

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.960
kind of like an approximate model built by
children from a few large lego pieces.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.690
The link to this work is available in the
video description.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:05.420
But clearly, we can do better, so how could
this be possible?

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:10.550
Well, the most important observation is that
if we take a photograph of a room, there is

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:16.040
a high chance that the furniture within are
not custom built, but mostly commercially

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:17.600
available pieces.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:21.220
So who said that we have to build these models
from scratch?

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:25.980
Let's look into a database that contains the
geometry for publicly available furniture

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:30.130
pieces and find which ones are seen in the
image!

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:35.729
So here's what we do: given a large amount
of training samples, neural networks are adept

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:38.700
at recognizing objects on a photograph.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.360
That would be step number one.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:46.130
After the identification, the algorithm knows
where the object is, now we're interested

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.330
in what it looks like and how it is aligned.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:54.440
And then, we start to look up public furniture
databases for objects that are as similar

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.729
to the ones presented in the photo as possible.

00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:03.049
Finally, we put everything in its appropriate
place, and create a new digital image with

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:04.950
a light simulation program.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:10.849
This is an iterative algorithm, which means
that it starts out with a coarse initial guess

00:02:10.849 --> 00:02:16.989
that is being refined many many times until
some sort of convergence is reached.

00:02:16.989 --> 00:02:21.849
Convergence means that no matter how hard
we try, only minor improvements can be made

00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:23.099
to this solution.

00:02:23.099 --> 00:02:24.989
Then, we can stop.

00:02:24.989 --> 00:02:30.159
And here, the dissimilarity between the photograph
and the digitally rendered image was subject

00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:31.579
to minimization.

00:02:31.579 --> 00:02:37.939
This entire process of creating the 3D geometry
of the scene takes around 5 minutes.

00:02:37.939 --> 00:02:43.340
And this technique can also estimate the layout
of a room from this one photograph.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:49.510
Now, this algorithm is absolutely amazing,
but of course, the limitations are also to

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:51.029
be candidly discussed.

00:02:51.029 --> 00:02:56.249
While some failure cases arise from misjudging
the alignment of the objects, the technique

00:02:56.249 --> 00:02:58.319
is generally quite robust.

00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:04.170
Non-cubic room shapes are also likely to introduce
issues such as the omission or misplacement

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.219
of an object.

00:03:05.219 --> 00:03:08.500
Also, kitchens and bathrooms are not yet supported.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:13.010
Note that this is not the only paper solving
this problem, I've made sure to link some

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:16.790
more related papers in the video description
for your enjoyment.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:21.419
If you have found this interesting, make sure
to subscribe and stay tuned for more Two Minute

00:03:21.419 --> 00:03:22.689
Papers episodes!

00:03:22.689 --> 00:03:27.009
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

